{
  "esm": "0.6.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "lambert_conformal",
    "description": "RFC pure-io-data-loaders §5.1 + Appendix B (bead esd-47z.2): the spherical Lambert Conformal Conic projection, authored as a plain declarative .esm over the EXISTING expression operator set (sin/cos/tan/atan/atan2/log/^/sqrt/sign + the built-in `pi` op) with NO new rule-schema, scaffold, validation, resolver, or per-binding code — exactly the longlat.esm template (esd-47z.1) it mirrors. The projection is PARAMETERIZED by {lat_1, lat_2, lat_0, lon_0, R} (scalar `parameter`s driven via parameter_overrides, as the regridder drives dx/dy/atol), and the FORWARD (geographic→projected) and INVERSE (projected→geographic) maps are SCALAR `observed`s defined by ordinary equations over the operator set — a coordinate transform is a point-wise scalar formula (the engine's tree-walk evaluator admits array-shaped parameters/observeds only under an active geometry/value-invention kernel; a pure transform has neither, so the declarative form is the scalar kernel a binding applies over a coordinate array). FORWARD (Appendix B), inputs in degrees, x/y in metres: n = log(cos φ₁/cos φ₂)/log(tan(π/4+φ₂/2)/tan(π/4+φ₁/2)); F = cos φ₁·tan(π/4+φ₁/2)^n/n; ρ = R·F/tan(π/4+φ/2)^n; ρ₀ = R·F/tan(π/4+φ₀/2)^n; x = ρ·sin(n(λ−λ₀)); y = ρ₀ − ρ·cos(n(λ−λ₀)). INVERSE (Appendix B), closed form: ρ = sign(n)·sqrt(x²+(ρ₀−y)²); θ = atan2(x, ρ₀−y); λ = λ₀ + θ/n; φ = 2·atan((R·F/ρ)^(1/n)) − π/2. The inverse observeds compose on the forward observeds (rho0_my, rho_inv, theta_inv read proj_x/proj_y), so inverse∘forward is the identity BY CONSTRUCTION — the round-trip the acceptance gate checks. Each map is surfaced through build_evaluator via the regridder's zero-IC state idiom: a state whose D-equation is d(state)/dt = <observed> from a zero IC, so du = f!(u0) IS the map value. forward_x/forward_y surface the forward map (checked against independently-computed proj4/Snyder reference points); roundtrip_lon/roundtrip_lat surface the round-trip (checked == input). The cone constant n depends only on the standard parallels, so the WRF (30/60/38.999996/−97/6370000, n≈0.7156) and NEI2016 (33/45/40/−97/6370997, n≈0.6305) parameter sets flow through this ONE rule with different outputs — the declarative-or-fail proof that the .esm format binds a genuinely parameterized transform, not a hard-coded constant. Each observed carries both a schema-canonical `expression` (read by the non-Julia bindings) and a mirrored `equation` (read by the non-geometry tree-walk evaluator, whose expression→equation synthesis is geometry/value-invention-gated, RFC §8.1). NO NEW PHYSICAL OPERATOR: composes from +,−,*,/,^,sin,cos,tan,atan,atan2,log,sqrt,sign,pi over scalar parameter/observed/state — all already landed. The second member of the reprojection/ family after longlat, and the first with a non-trivial (non-identity) forward/inverse pair.",
    "authors": [
      "earthscidiscretizations/gastown.furiosa"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "reprojection",
      "coordinate-transform",
      "lambert-conformal-conic",
      "lcc",
      "spherical",
      "projected-crs",
      "wrf",
      "nei2016",
      "forward-inverse",
      "round-trip",
      "parameterized-transform",
      "observed",
      "declarative",
      "pure-io-data-loaders",
      "esd-47z.2"
    ]
  },
  "models": {
    "LambertConformal": {
      "variables": {
        "lon": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Input geographic longitude (degrees) of the point being projected — the λ of the forward map. Scalar parameter driven via parameter_overrides; a binding applies the kernel over a coordinate array at the runtime layer (the longlat precedent)."
        },
        "lat": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Input geographic latitude (degrees) of the point being projected — the φ of the forward map."
        },
        "lat_1": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "First standard parallel (degrees), PROJ `+lat_1`. WRF=30, NEI2016=33. A projection PARAMETER — the two datasets drive the one rule with different values."
        },
        "lat_2": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Second standard parallel (degrees), PROJ `+lat_2`. WRF=60, NEI2016=45."
        },
        "lat_0": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Latitude of origin (degrees), PROJ `+lat_0`. WRF=38.999996, NEI2016=40. The latitude that maps to northing y=0."
        },
        "lon_0": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Central meridian / longitude of origin (degrees), PROJ `+lon_0`. Both datasets use −97. Maps to easting x=0."
        },
        "R": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Sphere radius (metres), PROJ `+R` (the WRF/NEI ellipsoid is spherical, +a=+b). WRF=6370000, NEI2016=6370997. Sets the absolute scale of x,y."
        },
        "deg2rad": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Degrees→radians conversion factor π/180. proj4/PROJ takes geographic coordinates and standard parallels in DEGREES (the WRF/NEI2016 +lat_1/+lat_2/+lat_0/+lon_0 values are degrees); Appendix B's trig is in radians. A constant observed (π is the built-in `pi` op); inlined wherever an angle is converted.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "/",
            "args": [
              3.141592653589793,
              180.0
            ]
          }
        },
        "rad2deg": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Radians→degrees conversion factor 180/π — the inverse of deg2rad. Converts the closed-form inverse's radian longitude/latitude back to degrees so the round-trip lands in the same units as the input.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "/",
            "args": [
              180.0,
              3.141592653589793
            ]
          }
        },
        "phi1": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "First standard parallel φ₁ in radians (lat_1·π/180). Appendix B's φ₁; drives the cone constant n and the scale factor F.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "*",
            "args": [
              "lat_1",
              "deg2rad"
            ]
          }
        },
        "phi2": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Second standard parallel φ₂ in radians (lat_2·π/180). Appendix B's φ₂; with φ₁ it fixes the cone constant n.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "*",
            "args": [
              "lat_2",
              "deg2rad"
            ]
          }
        },
        "phi0": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Origin latitude φ₀ in radians (lat_0·π/180). Sets ρ₀, the radius of the latitude that maps to the projection's false origin y=0.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "*",
            "args": [
              "lat_0",
              "deg2rad"
            ]
          }
        },
        "phi": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Input latitude φ in radians (lat·π/180) — the point being projected.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "*",
            "args": [
              "lat",
              "deg2rad"
            ]
          }
        },
        "t1": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Conformal tangent at φ₁: tan(π/4 + φ₁/2). Appears in both n and F; named once so the deep chain stays readable.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "tan",
            "args": [
              {
                "op": "+",
                "args": [
                  {
                    "op": "/",
                    "args": [
                      3.141592653589793,
                      4.0
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "op": "/",
                    "args": [
                      "phi1",
                      2.0
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Conformal tangent at φ₂: tan(π/4 + φ₂/2). The φ₂ term of the cone constant.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "tan",
            "args": [
              {
                "op": "+",
                "args": [
                  {
                    "op": "/",
                    "args": [
                      3.141592653589793,
                      4.0
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "op": "/",
                    "args": [
                      "phi2",
                      2.0
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "t0": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Conformal tangent at the origin latitude: tan(π/4 + φ₀/2). Sets ρ₀.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "tan",
            "args": [
              {
                "op": "+",
                "args": [
                  {
                    "op": "/",
                    "args": [
                      3.141592653589793,
                      4.0
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "op": "/",
                    "args": [
                      "phi0",
                      2.0
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "tphi": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Conformal tangent at the input latitude: tan(π/4 + φ/2). Sets ρ for the point. Singular (→0, ρ→∞) at the far pole φ=−90°·sign(n); the projection is undefined there by construction, so points must avoid that pole.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "tan",
            "args": [
              {
                "op": "+",
                "args": [
                  {
                    "op": "/",
                    "args": [
                      3.141592653589793,
                      4.0
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "op": "/",
                    "args": [
                      "phi",
                      2.0
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "n": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Cone constant n = log(cos φ₁ / cos φ₂) / log( tan(π/4+φ₂/2) / tan(π/4+φ₁/2) ) (Appendix B). Depends only on the two standard parallels, so it differs between the WRF (≈0.7156) and NEI2016 (≈0.6305) parameter sets — this is what makes the rule genuinely parameterized rather than hard-coded.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "/",
            "args": [
              {
                "op": "log",
                "args": [
                  {
                    "op": "/",
                    "args": [
                      {
                        "op": "cos",
                        "args": [
                          "phi1"
                        ]
                      },
                      {
                        "op": "cos",
                        "args": [
                          "phi2"
                        ]
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "op": "log",
                "args": [
                  {
                    "op": "/",
                    "args": [
                      "t2",
                      "t1"
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "F": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "Scale factor F = cos φ₁ · tan(π/4+φ₁/2)^n / n (Appendix B). The constant that ties the cone to the sphere; combined with R it sets every radius.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "/",
            "args": [
              {
                "op": "*",
                "args": [
                  {
                    "op": "cos",
                    "args": [
                      "phi1"
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "op": "^",
                    "args": [
                      "t1",
                      "n"
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              "n"
            ]
          }
        },
        "RF": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "R·F — the radius scale in metres (R is the sphere radius). A convenience observed: ρ, ρ₀ and the inverse latitude all read R·F, so naming it keeps those three simple.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "*",
            "args": [
              "R",
              "F"
            ]
          }
        },
        "rho0": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "ρ₀ = R·F / tan(π/4+φ₀/2)^n (Appendix B). Radius from the cone apex to the origin parallel; the y-axis is measured down from here.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "/",
            "args": [
              "RF",
              {
                "op": "^",
                "args": [
                  "t0",
                  "n"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "rho": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "ρ = R·F / tan(π/4+φ/2)^n (Appendix B). Radius from the cone apex to the input latitude.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "/",
            "args": [
              "RF",
              {
                "op": "^",
                "args": [
                  "tphi",
                  "n"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "theta": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "θ = n·(λ−λ₀), the polar angle on the cone, with the longitude difference (lon−lon_0) converted to radians. Appendix B writes x = ρ·sin(n(λ−λ₀)).",
          "expression": {
            "op": "*",
            "args": [
              "n",
              {
                "op": "*",
                "args": [
                  {
                    "op": "-",
                    "args": [
                      "lon",
                      "lon_0"
                    ]
                  },
                  "deg2rad"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "proj_x": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "FORWARD map, easting (metres): x = ρ·sin θ (Appendix B). At lon=lon_0, θ=0 ⇒ x=0 (the central meridian is the y-axis).",
          "expression": {
            "op": "*",
            "args": [
              "rho",
              {
                "op": "sin",
                "args": [
                  "theta"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "proj_y": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "FORWARD map, northing (metres): y = ρ₀ − ρ·cos θ (Appendix B). At (lon_0,lat_0): θ=0 and ρ=ρ₀ ⇒ y=0 (the projection origin maps to the false origin (0,0)).",
          "expression": {
            "op": "-",
            "args": [
              "rho0",
              {
                "op": "*",
                "args": [
                  "rho",
                  {
                    "op": "cos",
                    "args": [
                      "theta"
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "rho0_my": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "ρ₀ − y, the northing measured up from the cone apex. Composes on the FORWARD observed proj_y, so the inverse∘forward is the identity BY CONSTRUCTION (the longlat precedent: the inverse reads the forward observeds). Equals ρ·cos θ. Used by both ρ (inverse) and θ (inverse).",
          "expression": {
            "op": "-",
            "args": [
              "rho0",
              "proj_y"
            ]
          }
        },
        "rho_inv": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "INVERSE: ρ = sign(n)·sqrt(x² + (ρ₀−y)²) (Appendix B). Recovers the radius from the projected point; sign(n) keeps ρ on the correct side for either hemisphere's cone. For the round-trip this equals the forward ρ.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "*",
            "args": [
              {
                "op": "sign",
                "args": [
                  "n"
                ]
              },
              {
                "op": "sqrt",
                "args": [
                  {
                    "op": "+",
                    "args": [
                      {
                        "op": "^",
                        "args": [
                          "proj_x",
                          2.0
                        ]
                      },
                      {
                        "op": "^",
                        "args": [
                          "rho0_my",
                          2.0
                        ]
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "theta_inv": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "INVERSE: θ = atan2(x, ρ₀−y) (Appendix B). Recovers the polar angle; atan2(ρ sinθ, ρ cosθ)=θ for ρ>0, so it inverts the forward θ.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "atan2",
            "args": [
              "proj_x",
              "rho0_my"
            ]
          }
        },
        "geo_lon": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "INVERSE longitude (degrees): λ = λ₀ + θ/n, converted back to degrees: geo_lon = lon_0 + (θ_inv/n)·(180/π). inverse∘forward = lon_0 + (n(lon−lon_0))/n = lon — the round-trip identity the acceptance checks.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "+",
            "args": [
              "lon_0",
              {
                "op": "*",
                "args": [
                  {
                    "op": "/",
                    "args": [
                      "theta_inv",
                      "n"
                    ]
                  },
                  "rad2deg"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "geo_lat": {
          "type": "observed",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "INVERSE latitude (degrees): φ = 2·atan( (R·F/ρ)^(1/n) ) − π/2 (Appendix B), converted to degrees. Since R·F/ρ = tan(π/4+φ/2)^n, this returns the input latitude: inverse∘forward = φ. The round-trip identity in latitude.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "*",
            "args": [
              {
                "op": "-",
                "args": [
                  {
                    "op": "*",
                    "args": [
                      2.0,
                      {
                        "op": "atan",
                        "args": [
                          {
                            "op": "^",
                            "args": [
                              {
                                "op": "/",
                                "args": [
                                  "RF",
                                  "rho_inv"
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "op": "/",
                                "args": [
                                  1.0,
                                  "n"
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          }
                        ]
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "op": "/",
                    "args": [
                      3.141592653589793,
                      2.0
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              "rad2deg"
            ]
          }
        },
        "forward_x": {
          "type": "state",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "SURFACING state for the forward easting: d(forward_x)/dt = proj_x from a zero IC ⇒ du = x. Lets the gate read the forward output and compare to the proj4 reference points."
        },
        "forward_y": {
          "type": "state",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "SURFACING state for the forward northing: d(forward_y)/dt = proj_y ⇒ du = y."
        },
        "roundtrip_lon": {
          "type": "state",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "SURFACING state for the inverse∘forward longitude: d(roundtrip_lon)/dt = geo_lon ⇒ du = lon_0 + (n(lon−lon_0))/n = lon. Acceptance: roundtrip_lon == lon."
        },
        "roundtrip_lat": {
          "type": "state",
          "shape": [],
          "description": "SURFACING state for the inverse∘forward latitude: d(roundtrip_lat)/dt = geo_lat ⇒ du = lat. Acceptance: roundtrip_lat == lat."
        }
      },
      "equations": [
        {
          "_comment": "Degrees→radians conversion factor π/180. proj4/PROJ takes geographic coordinates and standard parallels in DEGREES (the WRF/NEI2016 +lat_1/+lat_2/+lat_0/+lon_0 values are degrees); Appendix B's trig is in radians. A constant observed (π is the built-in `pi` op); inlined wherever an angle is converted.",
          "lhs": "deg2rad",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "/",
            "args": [
              3.141592653589793,
              180.0
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "_comment": "Radians→degrees conversion factor 180/π — the inverse of deg2rad. Converts the closed-form inverse's radian longitude/latitude back to degrees so the round-trip lands in the same units as the input.",
          "lhs": "rad2deg",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "/",
            "args": [
              180.0,
              3.141592653589793
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "_comment": "First standard parallel φ₁ in radians (lat_1·π/180). Appendix B's φ₁; drives the cone constant n and the scale factor F.",
          "lhs": "phi1",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "*",
            "args": [
              "lat_1",
              "deg2rad"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "_comment": "Second standard parallel φ₂ in radians (lat_2·π/180). Appendix B's φ₂; with φ₁ it fixes the cone constant n.",
          "lhs": "phi2",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "*",
            "args": [
              "lat_2",
              "deg2rad"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "_comment": "Origin latitude φ₀ in radians (lat_0·π/180). Sets ρ₀, the radius of the latitude that maps to the projection's false origin y=0.",
          "lhs": "phi0",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "*",
            "args": [
              "lat_0",
              "deg2rad"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "_comment": "Input latitude φ in radians (lat·π/180) — the point being projected.",
          "lhs": "phi",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "*",
            "args": [
              "lat",
              "deg2rad"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "_comment": "Conformal tangent at φ₁: tan(π/4 + φ₁/2). Appears in both n and F; named once so the deep chain stays readable.",
          "lhs": "t1",
          "rhs": {
